        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new step is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit.** The notification lives in its own `NotifyOwnerWelcome` step rather than being bolted onto `AuditOwnerCreated`. The two are genuinely distinct concerns (the `NOTIFY` logger vs. the `AUDIT` logger), so folding them together would have started turning the audit class into a catch-all. Keeping them separate is the correct altitude.\n- **Mirrors the established pattern exactly.** Same package, same `private static final Logger X = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"NAME\")` idiom, same `public void service(@Val Owner owner)` signature as the adjacent `AuditOwnerCreated` \u2014 a maintainer will find it immediately familiar.\n- **Wired correctly.** Inserted as `audit \u2192 notifyWelcome \u2192 respond`, so it runs after persistence (id assigned) and before the responder, consistent with the \"runs once the owner has been persisted\" Javadoc and the surrounding step chain. No governance is needed since it's a read-only side effect, matching `audit` which also carries no `govern`.\n- No duplication introduced; the class does one thing.\n\nOne non-blocking observation (semantics, not structure, so it doesn't change the verdict): `AuditOwnerCreated` deliberately routes its structured `memberId` through `OwnerPrimaryIdentifier.of(owner)` (\"the unified memberId\"), whereas this step reads `owner.getMemberId()` directly. That matches the audit class's *human-readable* line, so it's a defensible consistency choice \u2014 just worth a glance to confirm the plain getter is the intended identifier here rather than the unified one."
